          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong Freyd category
            is one way to axiomatize models of
            %span.newWikiWord
              call-by-value
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/call-by-value"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+languages"} programming languages
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+in+computer+science"} monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕍
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
            that models values and another category with the same
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            that models computations.
          %p
            Freyd categories thus provide a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            for typed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+languages"} programming languages
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/side+effects"} side effects
            such as memory access or printing. This is in the spirit of the discussion at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} typed
            term (i.e., program expression)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma\vdash t \colon X
            is interpreted as a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \to X
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕍
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/premonoidal+categories"} premonoidal categories
          %p
            Freyd categories resolve the following complaint about using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli categories
            to model impure effects in programming languages. The Kleisli category for a monad presumes the existence of some slightly higher-order types, since if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is an object then so is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(X)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(X)
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong Freyd category
            (following
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Levy04"} Levy 04
          %ul
            %li
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕍
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
              with
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
            %li
              a category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+with+the+same+collection+of+objects"} has the same objects
              as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕍
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
              = succeed ";" do
                %sup#fnref:1
                  %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
            %li
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕍
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              (with the finite products providing a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal structure
              for
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕍
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
            %li
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity-on-objects+functor"} identity-on-objects functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝕍
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J: \mathbb{V} \to \mathbb{C}
              that preserves the actions.
          %p
            An alternative but equivalent definition is as follows (following
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#PH99"} Power, Thielecke
          %p A Freyd category is given by
          %ul
            %li
              a category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕍
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
              with finite products;
            %li
              a symmetric
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/premonoidal+category"} premonoidal category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+with+the+same+collection+of+objects"} has the same objects
              as
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕍
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
            %li
              an identity-on-objects functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝕍
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J: \mathbb{V} \to \mathbb{C}
              strictly preserving symmetric premonoidal structure, whose image lies in the centre of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
          %p
            These definitions arguably violate the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity-on-objects+functor"} identity-on-objects functor
            in the definitions. That is, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕍
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalent"} equivalent
            to some
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕍
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            is equivalent to some
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝕍
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J: \mathbb{V} \to \mathbb{C}
            is a Freyd category, there may be no way to make a Freyd category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕍
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}' \to \mathbb{C}'
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity-on-objects+functor"} identity-on-objects functor
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-category"} M-category
            for further discussion.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_strong_monads Relation to strong monads.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monad"} strong monad
            on a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕍
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
            with finite products, then the Kleisli category of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            forms a Freyd category and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            has a right adjoint.
          %p
            Conversely, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕍
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{V},\mathbb{C},J)
            is a Freyd category and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            has a right adjoint
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            of the monad
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi RJ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RJ
          %p
            In more detail, the passage from a strong monad to a Freyd category is as follows. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a strong monad on a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕍
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
            with finite products, then the Kleisli category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            has a premonoidal structure given by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Y
                  %mo =
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\otimes Y=X\times Y
            on objects and where for a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:Y\to Z
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            (i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:Y\to T(Z)
            in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕍
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⋊
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X\rtimes f):X\otimes Y\to X\otimes Z
            is given by the composite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} str
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times Y\xrightarrow f X\times T(Z) \xrightarrow {\mathrm{str}} T(X\times Z)
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕍
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⋉
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f\ltimes X):Y\otimes X\to Z\otimes X
            is given by the composite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} str
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\times X\xrightarrow f T(Z)\times X \xrightarrow {\mathrm{str}} T(Z\times X)
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕍
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative monad
          %h3#relation_to_lawvere_theories_and_props Relation to Lawvere theories and PROPs
          %p
            To give a small Freyd category is to give an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+Lawvere+theory"} enriched Lawvere theory
            relative to the empty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sound+doctrine"} sound doctrine
            where the enriching category is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed
          %p
            For example, if (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} FinSet
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝕋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{FinSet}^{\mathrm{op}}\to \mathbb{T}
            ) is an ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} FinSet
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{FinSet}\to \mathbb{T}^{\mathrm{op}}
          %p
            The Lawvere theory is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebraic+theory"} commutative
            if and only if the premonoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}^{\mathrm{op}}
            is in fact monoidal.
          %p
            A minor generalization of Freyd category allows
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕍
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
            to be symmetric monoidal rather than cartesian monoidal. Then a commutative Freyd category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} FinSet
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} bij
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{FinSet}_{\mathrm{bij}}\to \mathbb{C}
            is the same thing as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PROP"} PROP
          %h3#relation_to_monads_on_presheaf_categories Relation to monads on presheaf categories
          %p
            This relates to the situation with monads as follows. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a strong monad on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi 𝕍
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{\mathbb{V}}
            then the Kleisli category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi 𝕍
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi 𝕍
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{\mathbb{V}}\to (\hat{\mathbb{V}})^T
            is a Freyd category. But also the identity-on-objects/full-and-faithful factorization of the composite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕍
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi 𝕍
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi 𝕍
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}\to \hat{\mathbb{V}}\to (\hat{\mathbb{V}})^T
            yields a Freyd category over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕍
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
          %p
            Every Freyd category arises in this way, giving a correspondence between colimit-preserving strong monads on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi 𝕍
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{\mathbb{V}}
            and Freyd categories over a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕍
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
            with products.
          %p
            On the other hand, a colimit-preserving monad on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi 𝕍
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{\mathbb{V}}
            is the
            %a{:href => "profunctor#FuncsOnPresheaves"} same thing as
            a monad in the category of profunctors whose carrier is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕍
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/monad#other_examples"} same thing as
            an identity on objects functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕍
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}\to\mathbb{C}
          %h2#etymology Etymology
          %p
            The terminology “Freyd category” is not explained in the papers in which it is introduced. In private communication with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Power"} John Power
          %blockquote
            %p In Spring 1989, Peter spent a sabbatical at Carnegie Mellon Uni in Pittsburgh. I was at Case Western Reserve Uni in Cleveland and hired a car each week to travel to Carnegie Mellon to attend Peter’s seminars, chat with him, and attend the regular weekly seminar.
            %p He began to introduce Axiomatic Domain Theory, the leading example being the relationship between the category of omega-cpos and strict maps between them and the category with the same objects but with partial maps. For instance, he noted that the former is cartesian closed, there is an inclusion into the latter, which has a right adjoint, and I vaguely recall he noted the symmetric monoidal structure on the latter, extending finite products.
            %p A little earlier, Eugenio Moggi extended his thesis work on partiality by proposing monads.
            %p So the combination of the two ideas inspired the notion of Freyd-category
          %h2#related_concepts Related Concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrow"} arrow
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+in+computer+science"} monad in computer science
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thunk-force+category"} thunk-force category
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/premonoidal+category"} premonoidal category
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duploid"} duploid
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd+multicategory"} Freyd multicategory
          %h2#references References
          %p Freyd categories were first used in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Power"} John Power
              and Hayo Thielecke “Environments, Continuation Semantics and Indexed Categories”, Theoretical Aspects of Computer Software, Third International Symposium, TACS ‘97
          %p and named Freyd categories in
          %ul
            %li#PH99
              John Power and Hayo Thielecke, “Closed Freyd- and
              = succeed "-Categories\u201D," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi κ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
          %p and the longer journal version of that paper has more discussion:
          %ul
            %li
              Paul Blain Levy, John Power and Hayo Thielecke, “Modelling environments in call-by-value programming languages”, Inf. Comput. 185(2): 182-210, 2003
              %a{:href => "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0890540103000889"} DOI: 10.1016/S0890-5401(03)00088-9
          %p The definition with monoidal actions appears in
          %ul
            %li#Levy04 Paul Blain Levy, “Call-by-Push-Value. A Functional/Imperative Synthesis,” Semantic Structures in Computation 2, Springer, 2004; appendix B*
          %p The idea of extending Freyd categories to monads on presheaf categories appears in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Power"}> John Power
              , “Generic models for computational effects”, Theor. Comput. Sci. 364(2): 254-269 (2006)
          %p This also contains the observation that enriched Lawvere theories are examples of Freyd categories, but the precise correspondence between the concepts is given in
          %ul
            %li#Staton14
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sam+Staton"}> Sam Staton
              , 2014.
              = succeed "." do
                %em Freyd categories are Enriched Lawvere Theories
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.entcs.2014.02.010"} doi:10.1016/j.entcs.2014.02.010
              (free).
          %p
            The connection with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
            (a.k.a.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrows"} arrows
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bart+Jacobs"}> Bart Jacobs
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Heunen"} Chris Heunen
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://homepages.inf.ed.ac.uk/cheunen/publications/2008/arrows/arrows.pdf"} preprint pdf
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              Kazuyuki Asada. “Arrows are strong monads”. Proceedings of MSFP ‘10.
              %a{:href => "http://www-kb.is.s.u-tokyo.ac.jp/~asada/papers/arrStrMnd.pdf"} preprint pdf
          .footnotes
